 or B5-B7 or B7-B8. B7 connects with B6-B8 or B8-B9. B8 connects with B7-B9.\n\nConsidering tile efficiency: Discarding T1 reduces terminals and frees up a slot for a more useful tile. Alternatively, discarding B2 or B4 might be okay, but they have some potential. T2 is a simple and could pair or sequence, but with only one, it's not great. However, T1 is more isolated.\n\nAlso, looking at the game history: Many terminals and honors have been discarded (T8, J3, J2, T8, T3, F4, T5, F4, W7, J3, B1, F3, T9, F4, T9). This might indicate that other players are also discarding terminals/honors, so maybe All Simples is harder because B9 is a terminal in my hand. But I could change B9 into a kong if I draw the fourth, which would give more points. However, B9 being terminal breaks All Simples anyway.\n\nI need to aim for a hand that can reach 8 points. With B9 pung (1 point for Pung of Terminals or Honors), plus maybe All Chows (2 points) if I form four chows, but I have a pung, so not all chows. Alternatively, Half Flush if I focus on one suit, but my hand is mixed. Maybe Mixed Straight? I have W, T, B suits. Could try for Mixed Straight (8 points) if I get chows 123, 456, 789 in different suits. Currently, I have W2-W3-W4 (could be 234 in W), T1-T2 (could be 123 in T if I get T3), and B6-B7-B8 (could be 678 in B). That's promising: I need T3 for T1-T2-T3 (123), W2-W3-W4 is already 234, and B6-B7-B8 is 678. Then I'd need a pair and another meld. The fourth meld could be something else, like a pung or another chow. And I have B9 triplet which could be the pair? Actually, triplet is a pung, so that would be one meld. So my hand could be: Pung B9, Chow W2-W3-W4, Chow T1-T2-T3 (need T3), Chow B6-B7-B8. That would be three chows and one pung, plus a pair? Wait, I'd have 14 tiles: B9 triplet (3 tiles), three chows (9 tiles), total 12, need a pair (2 tiles). I currently have 11 tiles, so after discarding one, I'll have 10? Actually, I have 11 now, I need to discard one to have 10